# {{.Service}}

API (HTTP) service on CloudWeGo Hertz, generated by devkit.

```sh
make tools        # once per machine: hz + kitex + thriftgo
make gen          # regenerate the code that comes from the IDLs listed in idl.mk
make run          # conf/local.yaml: your machine, with the Nacos on it (the file says how to start one)
make test
curl 'http://127.0.0.1:{{.Port}}/ping?message=hi'
```

- The HTTP API is defined in the project's shared IDL repository
  (`../idl/{{.Service}}/{{.Service}}.thrift`): every method is a route, and an
  annotation such as `api.get="/user/:id"` says which. Change it there, run
  `make gen`, fill in the new function in `handler/`, and open a pull request in
  the IDL repository.
- What `make gen` writes: `hertz_gen/` (request and response structs),
  `router/` (the routes) and `kitex_gen/` (clients of the RPC services). None of
  it is committed; CI regenerates it. The exceptions are yours and are
  committed: `handler/` (hz adds an empty function for a method that is new and
  never touches the others) and `router/*/middleware.go` (middleware per route
  or group).
- To call an RPC service, add its IDL to `IDLS` in `idl.mk` (for example
  `user/user.thrift`), run `make gen`, declare the client in `deps/deps.go` and
  create it in `app/app.go`; the comment on `Setup` shows how. A handler uses it
  as `deps.User` and passes the `ctx` of the request on: it carries the trace_id
  to the RPC service. (Not `app.User`: a Hertz handler already has an `app`, the
  package of `*app.RequestContext`.)
- What is yours and what is the framework's: `app/`, `deps/`, `handler/`,
  `router/*/middleware.go`, `conf/*.yaml`, `idl.mk` and `go.mod` are yours and
  never rewritten. `cmd/{{.Service}}/main.go`, the Makefile, `.hz`, the CI files
  and the Dockerfile belong to the framework and are replaced by
  `devkit update`; do not edit them.
- Configuration is `conf/<APP_ENV>.yaml`; `${VAR}` placeholders are read from
  the environment. Four environments: `local` (no `APP_ENV`: your own machine,
  registered in the Nacos on it), `dev` (the shared development server), `uat`
  and `prod`. To work from your machine against the development server's Nacos,
  see `register: false` in `conf/local.yaml`; a laptop never registers there.
- Shared infrastructure (logging, Nacos, Redis, ...) comes from
  `{{.CommonModule}}`.
